+/* SIMUT override — a response's small writes leave together, a segment at a time.
+ *
+ * With LWIP_NETIF_TX_SINGLE_PBUF (lwipopts.h), tcp_write( ) gives every new
+ * segment a pbuf of a whole MSS, about 1.5 KB of lwIP's 16 KB heap however
+ * few bytes it carries, and the server's setNoDelay(true) (WebServerTemplate)
+ * sends every write as a segment of its own. Upstream wrote a response as one
+ * write for the header and three for each chunk: SIMUT's /api/status, a
+ * header, 7 chunks and the terminator in 1,039 B, went out as 24 segments, and
+ * the heap holds about ten of 1,540 B. Measured on the bench (2026-10-14, Pico
+ * W, GET /api/status every 0.2 s on one keep-alive connection, 1,000 replies):
+ * 24.2 segments sent and 4.4 tcp_write( ) refusals for want of memory
+ * (tcp.memerr) per reply, the TCP_SEG pool at a peak of 11 of 32 and never
+ * short. A refused write waits for the client's ACKs to free room, and in 3
+ * of those replies a wait outlasted the write timeout and cut the reply
+ * (shortWrites( )); what held the ACKs back was not measured, and a lost
+ * segment waiting for lwIP to retransmit it would. With this override, 6,000
+ * replies on the same bench took 1.2 segments each, and no write was refused
